\begin_body

\begin_layout Standard
The coefficient of determination or 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the most commonly used metric for validating poverty maps generated
 using machine-learning methods.
 The standard validation procedure can be summarized in four steps: (1)
 using survey data calculate a poverty measure for the geographic unit of
 interest (e.g., village or cluster) and merge the poverty measure with features
 from some passively-collected data source (e.g., mobile phone data or satellite
 imagery); (2) randomly split the dataset into training and testing subsamples;
 (3) fit a machine-learning model to the training data and use the model
 to predict poverty in the testing data; (4) calculate the out-of-sample
 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 by regressing the observed poverty measures from the testing data on the
 predicted poverty measures for the same geographic units.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Rather than relying on a single training-testing split of the data, many
 studies use cross-validation and report the average 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 across various training-testing splits.
 For examples of the above procedure, see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "jean2016combining"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "pokhriyal2017combining"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "steele2017mapping"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "lee2020high"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "yeh2020using"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, or 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chi2022microestimates"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Note that a few studies use a similar procedure, but fit the machine-learning
 model to household-level data and then aggregate the model's predictions
 to the geographic unit of interest 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "blumenstock2015predicting,hersh2021open,aiken2022machine"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 It is important to emphasize that the poverty measures used in this procedure
 are often direct, sample-based estimates rather than the true poverty measures
 for the regions of interest.
 In what follows, we critically assess this validation procedure and discuss
 some alternative validation metrics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{a}$
\end_inset

 denote the actual or true poverty indicator for the 
\begin_inset Formula $i^{th}$
\end_inset

 geographical entity.
 Further, let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{d}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{p}$
\end_inset

 denote the direct estimate and model-based prediction of the poverty indicator,
 respectively.
 The standard method for assessing the predictive performance of model-based
 poverty estimates calculates the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 from a regression like the following:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}^{d}=\alpha+\beta y_{i}^{p}+\varepsilon_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 are the regression coefficients and 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon$
\end_inset

 represents the error term.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Note that any regression of this form can only be estimated for the sampled
 regions with direct estimates.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 for the above regression can be written as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{d}^{2}=\frac{\sigma_{d}^{2}-\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}}{\sigma_{d}^{2}}\label{eq:r2direct}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the variance of the direct estimate and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 thus conveys information about how much of the variance of the direct estimate
 is explained by the model-based prediction.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While unbiased, direct estimates can be imprecise estimates of true poverty
 indicators 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "corral2020pull"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Ideally, one would assess the performance of model-based predictions on
 the basis of the true poverty indicators (
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{a}$
\end_inset

) themselves.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In similar fashion to the 
\emph on
linking model
\emph default
 used in Fay-Herrtiot models (Fay and Herriot XXXX) we assume that the true
 indicator (
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{a}$
\end_inset

) is linearly related to a vector of area-level covariates (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}^{a}=\mathbf{x}_{i}^{'}\beta+u_{i}\label{eq:true_fh}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{i}^{'}\beta$
\end_inset

 is the predicted poverty rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{p}=\mathbf{x}_{i}^{'}\beta$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 are the area effects since they represent unexplained heterogeneity between
 areas.
 However, we do not have the true area's poverty rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{a}$
\end_inset

.
 All we have are the direct estimates, which are centered around the true
 values:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}^{d}=y_{i}^{a}+\varepsilon{}_{i}\label{eq:direct_fh}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon_{i}$
\end_inset

 are the heteroskedastic errors with variances 
\begin_inset Formula $var(\varepsilon_{i}|y_{i}^{a})=\psi_{i}$
\end_inset

 and is called the sampling model, for simplicity we assume that homoskedastic
 errors and thus 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon$
\end_inset

 .
 Note that the sampling variances are assumed to vary across areas.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Replacing equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:true_fh"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:direct_fh"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 tells us that:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}^{d}=x'_{i}\beta+u_{i}+e_{i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consequently, the model's true 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
R^{2}=\frac{\sigma_{d}^{2}-\sigma_{u}^{2}-\sigma_{e}^{2}}{\sigma_{d}^{2}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However, note that in the validations normally done the sampling error,
 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

, is ignored.
 Assume that for a given country we have 2 samples of different sizes, consequen
tly the sampling error in the second sample will be larger.
 If everything else is equal, we would be led to believe that models conducted
 on both sets of data yield estimates of similar quality.
 However, the performance of the first model will be in reality better since
 it was performed on better quality data.
\end_layout

